# API Error Responses

Never return stack traces, SQL errors, file paths, env values, or other internal exception details to API clients. Log the full error server-side; respond with a generic, safe message and appropriate status code.

## Do / Don't

```typescript
// ❌ BAD — leaks internals to the client
res.status(500).json({ error: err.message, stack: err.stack });
res.status(500).send(err.toString());

// ✅ GOOD — generic client message; details stay in logs
logger.error("Request failed", { err, requestId });
res.status(500).json({
  error: "Internal server error",
  requestId, // safe correlation id only
});
```

## Checklist

- Catch unexpected errors in a central error handler; map known domain errors to stable client-facing codes/messages.
- Never forward raw `err.message`, ORM/driver errors, or upstream service payloads on 5xx responses.
- Include a request/correlation ID so support can look up the real error in logs.
- Keep 4xx validation messages actionable but free of secrets and implementation details.
